{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/15/prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n     2\t\n     3\t每个参考题描述包含：\n     4\t- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n     5\t- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n     6\t- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n     7\t- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n     8\t\n     9\t参考题目如下：\n    10\t<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"18286ef8\" lines=\"17-31\">\n    11\t<description>\n    12\tUse the pink control pixel to choose where the maroon pixel belongs inside the grey 3x3 region, and turn the control pixel maroon.\n    13\t\n    14\tInput structure:\n    15\t- The grid contains one 3x3 region made from eight grey pixels and one maroon pixel.\n    16\t- There is exactly one pink control pixel outside that region.\n    17\t\n    18\tRule:\n    19\t- Restore the old maroon cell in the 3x3 region to grey.\n    20\t- Compare the pink pixel to the 3x3 region: above/same band/below selects top/middle/bottom, and left/same band/right selects left/middle/right.\n    21\t- Paint the selected cell maroon and recolor the pink control pixel maroon.\n    22\t\n    23\tKey insight:\n    24\t- The pink pixel acts as a relative-position marker for a 3x3 target inside the grey region.\n    25\t</description>\n    26\t</reference_puzzle>\n    27\t\n    28\t<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n    29\t<description>\n    30\tComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n    31\t\n    32\tInput structure\n    33\t- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n    34\t- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n    35\t\n    36\tRule\n    37\t- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n    38\t- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n    39\t\n    40\tKey insight\n    41\t- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n    42\t</description>\n    43\t</reference_puzzle>\n    44\t\n    45\t<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n    46\t<description>\n    47\tSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n    48\t\n    49\tInput structure:\n    50\t- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n    51\t- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.\n    52\t- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.\n    53\t\n    54\tRule:\n    55\t- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.\n    56\t- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.\n    57\t- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).\n    58\t- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.\n    59\t\n    60\tKey insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.\n    61\t</description>\n    62\t</reference_puzzle>\n    63\t\n    64\t<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n    65\t<description>\n    66\tRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n    67\t\n    68\tInput structure\n    69\t- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n    70\t- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n    71\t- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n    72\t\n    73\tRule\n    74\t- Keep the background unchanged.\n    75\t- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n    76\t- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n    77\t\n    78\tKey insight\n    79\t- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n    80\t</description>\n    81\t</reference_puzzle>\n    82\t\n    83\t<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n    84\t<description>\n    85\tReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n    86\t\n    87\tInput structure:\n    88\t- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n    89\t- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n    90\t- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n    91\t\n    92\tRule:\n    93\t- Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n    94\t- Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n    95\t- In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n    96\t\n    97\tKey insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n    98\t</description>\n    99\t</reference_puzzle>\n   100\t\n   101\t<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"33067df9\" lines=\"32-48\">\n   102\t<description>\n   103\tExpand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.\n   104\t\n   105\tInput structure\n   106\t- The background is the dominant color.\n   107\t- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.\n   108\t- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.\n   109\t\n   110\tRule\n   111\t- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.\n   112\t- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.\n   113\t- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.\n   114\t- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.\n   115\t- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.\n   116\t\n   117\tKey insight\n   118\t- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.\n   119\t</description>\n   120\t</reference_puzzle>\n   121\t\n   122\t<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n   123\t<description>\n   124\tTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n   125\t\n   126\tInput structure:\n   127\t- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n   128\t- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n   129\t\n   130\tRule:\n   131\t- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n   132\t- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n   133\t- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n   134\t- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n   135\t\n   136\tKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n   137\t</description>\n   138\t</reference_puzzle>\n   139\t\n   140\t<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5e6bbc0b\" lines=\"42-56\">\n   141\t<description>\n   142\tCompact the checkerboard's moving color toward the edge marked by the teal pixel, leaving a maroon swept trail on the marker line.\n   143\t\n   144\tInput structure\n   145\t- A blue/black checkerboard with exactly one teal marker replacing a blue pixel on a non-corner edge.\n   146\t- The marker's checkerboard parity identifies the moving color (blue); the opposite parity is background (black).\n   147\t\n   148\tRule\n   149\t- If the marker is on the left or right edge, compact moving-color pixels in every row toward that side.\n   150\t- If the marker is on the top or bottom edge, compact moving-color pixels in every column toward that side.\n   151\t- Preserve the teal marker at its edge cell.\n   152\t- On the marker's row or column, color cells swept by shifted moving pixels maroon unless the final compacted line occupies them.\n   153\t\n   154\tKey insight\n   155\tThe marker selects both the compression direction and the checkerboard color class that moves; maroon records the movement trail only along that marked line.\n   156\t</description>\n   157\t</reference_puzzle>\n   158\t\n   159\t<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4888269\" lines=\"67-88\">\n   160\t<description>\n   161\tRecolor every target pixel by following the ordered two-column substitution legend.\n   162\t\n   163\tInput structure\n   164\t- A dominant background surrounds one multicolor vertical legend: a connected,\n   165\t  fully filled two-column block with one color pair per row.\n   166\t- Other non-background pixels or objects are the targets; divider or frame\n   167\t  pixels are ordinary target-side structure that remain unchanged unless their\n   168\t  color is rewritten by the legend.\n   169\t\n   170\tRule\n   171\t- Read the legend rows from top to bottom as substitutions from the left\n   172\t  column color to the right column color.\n   173\t- For each non-background pixel outside the legend, scan those rows in order;\n   174\t  whenever the current color equals the row's left color, replace it with the\n   175\t  row's right color and keep scanning lower rows.\n   176\t- Leave the legend itself and background pixels unchanged.\n   177\t\n   178\tKey insight\n   179\t- The same color may be rewritten multiple times, but only by substitutions\n   180\t  that appear later in the vertical legend, so the row order is part of the\n   181\t  symbol instruction.\n   182\t</description>\n   183\t</reference_puzzle>\n   184\t\n   185\t<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db7260a4\" lines=\"37-56\">\n   186\t<description>\n   187\tDrop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.\n   188\t\n   189\tInput structure\n   190\t- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,\n   191\t  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.\n   192\t- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous\n   193\t  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.\n   194\t\n   195\tRule\n   196\t- Remove the top marker from its original cell.\n   197\t- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its\n   198\t  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.\n   199\t- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row\n   200\t  with the marker color.\n   201\t\n   202\tKey insight\n   203\t- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop\n   204\t  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.\n   205\t</description>\n   206\t</reference_puzzle>\n   207\t\n   208\t<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f5f939b\" lines=\"42-57\">\n   209\t<description>\n   210\tMark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n   211\t\n   212\tInput structure:\n   213\t- The background is the dominant color.\n   214\t- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n   215\t- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n   216\t\n   217\tRule:\n   218\t- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n   219\t- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n   220\t- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n   221\t\n   222\tKey insight:\n   223\t- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.\n   224\t</description>\n   225\t</reference_puzzle>\n   226\t\n   227\t<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5587a8d0\" lines=\"11-27\">\n   228\t<description>\n   229\tRedraw foreground objects as nested square rings ordered by object size.\n   230\t\n   231\tInput structure:\n   232\t- The grid has one dominant background color.\n   233\t- Every foreground object is a 4-connected monochromatic component.\n   234\t- Foreground object areas are distinct.\n   235\t\n   236\tRule:\n   237\t- Sort the foreground objects by decreasing pixel count.\n   238\t- Create a square output with side length 2 * number_of_objects - 1.\n   239\t- The largest object color fills the outer ring, each next object color fills\n   240\t  the next inner ring, and the smallest object color fills the center pixel.\n   241\t\n   242\tKey insight: The objects' original shapes do not get copied; their areas\n   243\tdetermine only the outside-to-inside order of the nested square pattern.\n   244\t</description>\n   245\t</reference_puzzle>\n   246\t\n   247\t<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n   248\t<description>\n   249\tExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n   250\t\n   251\tInput structure:\n   252\t- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n   253\t- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n   254\t\n   255\tRule:\n   256\t- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n   257\t- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n   258\t- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n   259\t\n   260\tKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n   261\t</description>\n   262\t</reference_puzzle>\n   263\t\n   264\t<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342dd610\" lines=\"18-36\">\n   265\t<description>\n   266\tMove every foreground pixel by the fixed offset indicated by its color.\n   267\t\n   268\tInput structure:\n   269\t- The grid has a unique dominant background color.\n   270\t- The foreground is a sparse set of colored pixels.\n   271\t- Each foreground color is one of the trained motion colors: orange, red,\n   272\t  maroon, or blue.\n   273\t\n   274\tRule:\n   275\t- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n   276\t- Translate orange pixels up 2 cells, red pixels left 2 cells, maroon\n   277\t  pixels down 2 cells, and blue pixels right 1 cell.\n   278\t- Preserve each moved pixel's original color.\n   279\t\n   280\tKey insight:\n   281\tThe foreground color is the motion instruction; position only determines\n   282\twhere that color-specific displacement lands.\n   283\t</description>\n   284\t</reference_puzzle>\n   285\t\n   286\t<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\" lines=\"36-55\">\n   287\t<description>\n   288\tFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n   289\t\n   290\tInput structure:\n   291\t- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n   292\t  separator column.\n   293\t- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n   294\t- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n   295\t\n   296\tRule:\n   297\t- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n   298\t- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n   299\t  each color across the four regions.\n   300\t- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n   301\t  completed multiset.\n   302\t\n   303\tKey insight:\n   304\t- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n   305\t  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n   306\t</description>\n   307\t</reference_puzzle>\n   308\t\n   309\t<reference_puzzle index=\"16\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\" lines=\"82-97\">\n   310\t<description>\n   311\tRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n   312\t\n   313\tInput structure\n   314\t- The background is the dominant color.\n   315\t- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n   316\t- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n   317\t\n   318\tRule\n   319\t- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n   320\t- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n   321\t- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n   322\t\n   323\tKey insight\n   324\tThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n   325\t</description>\n   326\t</reference_puzzle>\n   327\t\n   328\t<reference_puzzle index=\"17\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\" lines=\"102-115\">\n   329\t<description>\n   330\tSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n   331\t\n   332\tInput structure:\n   333\t- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n   334\t- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n   335\t\n   336\tRule:\n   337\t- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n   338\t- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n   339\t- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n   340\t\n   341\tKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n   342\t</description>\n   343\t</reference_puzzle>\n   344\t\n   345\t<reference_puzzle index=\"18\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n   346\t<description>\n   347\tDrop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n   348\t\n   349\tInput structure:\n   350\t- The background is the dominant color.\n   351\t- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n   352\t- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n   353\t\n   354\tRule:\n   355\t- Clear the original sprite pixels.\n   356\t- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n   357\t  pixel it crossed.\n   358\t- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n   359\t  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n   360\t\n   361\tKey insight:\n   362\tThe red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\n   363\tbottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n   364\t</description>\n   365\t</reference_puzzle>\n   366\t\n   367\t<reference_puzzle index=\"19\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6d1d5c90\" lines=\"8-23\">\n   368\t<description>\n   369\tRemove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.\n   370\t\n   371\tInput structure\n   372\t- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.\n   373\t- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.\n   374\t- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.\n   375\t\n   376\tRule\n   377\t- Delete the left control column.\n   378\t- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.\n   379\t- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.\n   380\t\n   381\tKey insight\n   382\t- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink\n   383\t  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.\n   384\t</description>\n   385\t</reference_puzzle>\n   386\t\n   387\t<reference_puzzle index=\"20\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"afe3afe9\" lines=\"106-119\">\n   388\t<description>\n   389\tConvert each hollow 3x3 symbol to one pixel and slide the pixels clockwise from the marked edge.\n   390\t\n   391\tInput structure\n   392\t- The grid has a background, one full-edge marker line, and monochrome hollow 3x3 foreground symbols.\n   393\t- The symbols lie on a coarse row/column lattice; fully empty lattice rows or columns are absent from the output.\n   394\t\n   395\tRule\n   396\t- Replace each hollow 3x3 symbol with one pixel of its color in the corresponding coarse-grid cell.\n   397\t- The marker edge gives the compaction direction by clockwise border travel: top -> right, right -> down, bottom -> left, left -> up.\n   398\t- Preserve the order of non-background pixels while sliding them as far as possible in that direction.\n   399\t\n   400\tKey insight\n   401\t- The large picture is a scaled symbolic grid, and the blue edge marker acts as a gravity direction rather than as output content.\n   402\t</description>\n   403\t</reference_puzzle>\n   404\t\n   405\t你的任务：\n   406\t以参考题为标杆，运用发散性思维进行创新扩展。请发挥创造力，构思全新且巧妙的推理谜题。用中文创造全新的 ARC 谜题描述。\n   407\t创新方式包括但不限于：\n   408\t**机制重组**：将多道参考题各自的核心机制提取出来，组合成一个新的多步管道，用到新的场景。\n   409\t**机制变异**：取几道参考题的核心机制，以机制为基础进行创新并与其他多种机制进行创新重组，使解题路径发生质变而非量变。\n   410\t**逆向构造**：取一道参考题的输出模式，思考\"什么样的输入规则能产生类似的输出结构但需要完全不同的推理过程\"。\n   411\t\n   412\t严格使用下面的 XML 风格格式输出：\n   413\t\n   414\t<new_puzzle>\n   415\t- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n   416\t- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n   417\t- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n   418\t- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n   419\t</new_puzzle>\n   420\t\n   421\t请一次性生成 5 个 `<new_puzzle>`，若有较多合适的创新，也可以生成更多，不设上限，但不要输出额外解释。\n   422\t\n   423\t注意事项：\n   424\t1. 任务不应依赖大量先验知识或复杂计算，而应依赖对模式、结构与空间关系的直觉推理。\n   425\t2. 关于\"计算\"的边界：\n   426\t   - **允许使用**（这些都是人类能心算的视觉量）：数像素 / 孔洞 / 对象 / 邻居（≤10 量级）、比大小、按某个可见属性排序、奇偶判断、取最大 / 最小、用一个计数值作为另一个等量几何量（如 N 个像素 → 长度 N 的射线、面积 N → 边长 N）、判断接触 / 相邻 / 包含、粗略中心位置（簇的视觉中点）、\"距离\"作为\"几步\"而不是公式等人类能够直观感受计算的内容。\n   427\t   - **禁止使用**（这些是算法量而非视觉量，会迫使解题者依赖公式而非视觉直觉）：曼哈顿 / 欧氏 / Chebyshev 距离作为精确查表键、凸包面积、二进制位编码 / 位运算、模运算 / 余数、拓扑距离（BFS / 最短路 / 跳数）、精确几何质心坐标、凸角 / 凹角计数等复杂且不易被看出来的计算都是禁止使用的。\n   428\t3. 问题必须能从少量示例中推断出来（通常为 3 对训练样例 + 1 个测试样例）。设计时从人类解题者的视觉角度思考：规则涉及的所有关键要素（对象、关系、属性）必须在网格上肉眼可见，不应依赖人眼无法直接观察到的隐含信息（例如颜色id和颜色亮度是禁止使用的）或需要推算的隐藏属性。规则背后的逻辑本身可以有一定挑战性，但解题所需的所有线索必须能从网格中直接获取。\n   429\t4. 生成的问题描述旨在促进后续用于创建题目网格的代码生成。因此，对输入 / 输出网格的描述以及规则总结必须极其详细；语言风格应易于理解；对于特定对象（如箭头），必须在描述中明确其绘制方式。\n   430\t5. 描述谜题时避免使用 0 或 1 这类颜色编号；应使用颜色名称，例如 \"red\"。可用颜色如下：Black、Blue、Red、Green、Yellow、Grey、Magenta、Orange、Azure、Maroon。\n   431\t6. 5 个新题的核心推理机制应明显不同——即从观察样例到归纳规则的认知路径不同，而不仅仅是场景、对象或颜色不同。\n   432\t7. 题目难度应来自机制的隐蔽性而非步骤数。用参考题作为难度标尺——如果你生成的题一个样例就能猜到规则，说明机制太透明，需要加深隐蔽性。\n   433\t8. 优先从参考题的机制出发进行重组或推演，而非从物理隐喻出发。物理隐喻容易导致大量题目归结为一种模式，例如\"沿方向移动到阻挡处停止\"。\n   434\t9. 题目的难度优先来自规则的**结构层数**，而不是单一步骤的复杂度。关于\"结构层数\"的判定标准：**只有解题者需要主动推理才能完成的步骤才算一层**。肉眼一扫即可获得的信息（如\"找到缺口在哪一侧\"、\"数出像素个数\"、\"识别哪个对象最大\"）不构成独立的推理层——它们只是变换的前置输入，不增加认知难度。真正的多层结构是指每一层都需要解题者发现一个非显然的规律。以两层结构为例：\n   435\t   - ✗ 伪两层：\"找到缺口方向\"→\"把东西推到对面\" — 第一步肉眼可见，实际只有一层推理。\n   436\t   - ✓ 真两层：\"通过孔洞数量匹配颜色图例\"→\"用匹配到的颜色重绘对象\" — 两步都需要非平凡的归纳。\n   437\t\n   438\t   如果你发现自己写出的题只是\"对每个对象，根据它的某个属性做某变换\"的单步规则，请引入一个需要主动推理的前置步骤。**用堆叠 tie-break 规则或复杂数学公式来\"加难度\"是劣解；用多一层需要归纳的结构嵌套来\"加难度\"才是好解**。\n   439\t10. 在符合ARC题目和提示词要求的基础上，鼓励尝试创新规则为多层结构的题目，不局限于两层，可以创新三层或四层结构的题目，但不可为了多结构层数违背要求。\n   440\t\n   441\t生成后对生成的题执行以下四项审查（审查内容不需要保存到文件）：\n   442\t1. 原创性\n   443\t  新题不可与上述参考题相同或类似，不可只是替换颜色、方向、对象类型等表层要素。判断标准：新题的核心推理路径应与任何一道参考题不同。\n   444\t2. 确定性\n   445\t  规则完全确定，同一输入只能产生唯一输出，每一步操作都有明确定义。\n   446\t3. 可实现性\n   447\t  所有必要细节都已给出——对象如何生成、如何识别、如何变换、边界情况如何处理，读完描述后可以直接写代码。\n   448\t4. ARC 合规性\n   449\t  网格尺寸合理（≤30×30），使用颜色不超过 10 种，规则复杂度适中，人类能从 3-5 个样例中发现规律。没有使用上述注意事项第二条禁止使用的复杂计算。\n   450\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"约束已经够清楚了，我会按这个结构实现：\n\n1. `main(grid)`：\n- 先从角落图例读出 `边色种类数 -> 填充色` 映射。\n- 再找所有不接触外边界的 Azure 连通域。\n- 只接受“内部是实心矩形、四周一圈是线段边界”的区域，统计四条边的颜色种类数并填充。\n\n2. `generate_input()`：\n- 随机角落放图例，随机 2-4 个闭合矩形，四边颜色独立采样，覆盖 1-4 种边色分支。\n- 随机加入一部分未闭合线框作干扰，但保证不与图例或闭合区域粘连。\n- 用“重试直到通过 `main()` 回放验证”的方式防止退化输入。\n\n3. 完成后：\n- 把代码写到目标路径。\n- 跑你给的 100 次变体自检和 `py_compile`。\n- 清理 `__pycache__`。"}}
